  abstract-en: (
    content: [
With the wide deployment of multimodal large language models in image understanding, visual question answering, and intelligent assistants, their safety concerns have expanded from the pure-text space to the joint vision-language space. Existing studies on multimodal jailbreaks show that attackers can weaken safety alignment through typographic image embedding, visual perturbations, and image-text linkage prompts. However, current methods still suffer from over-exposed risk semantics, an image modality that mainly serves as a passive payload, and insufficient stability against advanced models.

To address these issues, this thesis takes multimodal large language models as the research object and proposes a cross-modal linkage jailbreak attack method based on image semantic embedding. The method decomposes the target semantics into fine-grained fragments recognizable on the image side, and introduces encoded clues, reconstruction and verification steps, and structured output constraints on the text side, so that the image is transformed from a passive carrier into an active semantic prompt factor that guides the model's attention and task interpretation.

Experiments conducted on 250 samples from five sensitive task categories in SafeBench show that the complete method achieves average attack success rates of 87.60%, 95.60%, and 93.20% on gpt-4o, grok-3, and qwen-vl-max, with average query numbers of 1.96, 1.37, and 1.65, respectively, significantly outperforming the FigStep baseline (ASR / QN of 33.20%/3.85, 47.20%/3.21, and 42.00%/3.49). Ablation studies on gpt-4o further show that removing the Base64 encoding guidance, the image-side PGD semantic embedding, the multi-step reconstruction chain, the structured output template, or the malicious semantic alignment results in ASR drops of 11.60 to 16.80 percentage points, verifying the necessity of each component. These results indicate that the combination of image semantic cues and the textual reconstruction chain can substantially improve the parseability and triggering efficiency of attack inputs, and also expose notable weaknesses of current multimodal systems in cross-modal hidden semantics and multi-step reconstruction tasks. This work provides a reference for safety evaluation and robust defense design of multimodal large language models.
    ],
